      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Louisville and Nashville Railroad built a depot in 1859, calling it 'New Woodburn' before dropping the name by 1864.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodburn%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Woodburn's post office opened in 1843, was renamed New Woodburn in 1859, and returned to Woodburn in 1866.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://kyatlas.com/ky-woodburn.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The oldest grave in Woodburn Cemetery dates back to 1854, marking the town's early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
